Build native CUDA code for Turing (sm_75) GPUs.

ovphysx-0.5.11's GPU arch list targets SASS for sm_80..sm_120 (Ampere and
newer) plus a compute_120 PTX fallback -- nothing for sm_75 (Turing:
RTX 20-series, GTX 16-series, Quadro RTX). On those cards every PhysX CUDA
kernel is JIT-compiled by the driver from the newest-ISA PTX down to
Turing at load, which produces slow code: GPU rigid-body stepping runs
~8x slower than native (25k boxes: ~180 ms/step instead of ~23 ms).

Add sm_75 to both the reduced and full SASS lists so Turing gets a native
cubin. Costs a little CUDA compile time; no runtime downside on other
archs.

diff --git a/physx/source/compiler/cmakegpu/CMakeLists.txt b/physx/source/compiler/cmakegpu/CMakeLists.txt
index f2e5c86..da02fbe 100644
--- a/physx/source/compiler/cmakegpu/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/physx/source/compiler/cmakegpu/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -94,10 +94,10 @@ SET(SOURCE_DISTRO_FILE_LIST "")
 # since that's the default set in: compiler\internal\CMakeLists.txt (using CMAKE_CUDA_ARCHITECTURES)
 # see policy CMP0104
 IF(PX_GENERATE_GPU_REDUCED_ARCHITECTURES)
-	GENERATE_ARCH_CODE_LIST(SASS "80,86,89,90,100,120" PTX "120")
+	GENERATE_ARCH_CODE_LIST(SASS "75,80,86,89,90,100,120" PTX "120")
 ELSE()
 	# Volta is the minimum compute arch required because NGC supports V100
-	GENERATE_ARCH_CODE_LIST(SASS "70,80,86,89,90,100,120" PTX "120")
+	GENERATE_ARCH_CODE_LIST(SASS "70,75,80,86,89,90,100,120" PTX "120")
 ENDIF()

 # Force response files off because clangd does not parse them
